  "content": {
    "id": "p27-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "The product $\\dfrac{x^2+bx+c}{x^2-9}\\times\\dfrac{x-3}{x+2}$ simplifies to $\\dfrac{x+5}{x+2}$. Find $b$ and $c$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$b=8$, $c=15$.",
      "worked": "Since $x^2-9=(x-3)(x+3)$, cancelling $x-3$ leaves $x+3$ in the denominator. The numerator must therefore be $(x+3)(x+5)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+bx+c&=(x+3)(x+5)\\\\&=x^2+8x+15\\end{align*}$$\nComparing coefficients gives $b=8$ and $c=15$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 42
  },
